ion of “computable”. In his famous 1936 paper Turing\nL17@P0: [Tur1] formulated the idea of the universal digital computer, the Turing machine.\nL18@P0: It is not necessary to have in mind the exact details of how Turing machines\nL19@P0: work and one can think of such a machine as an ordinary computer with a\nL20@P0: xed\nL21@P0: program but with in\nL22@P0: nite memory and no time limit on computations. \u0018e crucial\nL23@P0: fact is that a Turing machine is completely de\nL24@P0: ned by its\nL25@P0: nite program and that\nL202@P4: this notion because he was able to solve Post’s problem for truth-table degrees\nL203@P4: by showing that there is a c.e. set B with 0 <t t B <t t K . It turns out that\nL204@P4: the structure of truth-table degrees within a single Turing degree is very rich.\nL205@P4: Jockusch [Joc] showed that any Turing degree either contains in\nL206@P4: nitely many\nL207@P4: truth-table-degrees or is itself a single truth-table degree, and this later case is\nL208@P4: very rare in a precise sense. Indeed, Degtev [Deg] showed that any nonzero\nL209@P4: c.e. Turing degree contains in\nL210@P4: nitely many pairwise incomparable c.e. truth-table\nL211@P4: degrees.\nL212@P4: As with Turing degrees, any set is truth-table equivalent to its complement and\nL213@P4: a truth-table degree is said to be a c.e. degree if it contains a c.e. set. We have\nL214@P4: pointed out that the computational complexity of the word problem of a\nL215@P4: nitely\nL216@P4: generated group is an invariant of the group and does not depend on a given\nL217@P4: \nL218@P4: nitely generated presentation. In discussing the diversity of\nL219@P4: nitely generated\nL220@P4: subgroups of C , we need the fact that the truth-table degree of the word problem\nL221@P4: of a\nL222@P4-5: nitely generated group G is an invariant of the group.148 A. Morozov and P. Schupp\nL223@P5: 3. HNN extensions\nL224@P5: A basic construction in in\nL225@P5: nite group theory is that of taking an HNN\nL2